      ],
      "source": "https://www.aberdeenwa.gov/257/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from a local salmon cannery and echoes Aberdeen, Scotland, its namesake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aberdeenwa.gov/257/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1895 residents built a spur that linked Aberdeen to the Northern Pacific Railroad, boosting regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aberdeenwa.gov/257/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Aberdeen Gardens hosts the Grays Harbor Historical Seaport, which operates the Lady Washington, a replica of the first American ship to visit the West Coast.",
